GDPR Audit Pack — Elza Paegle

Audit worksheet

Transition note from the EU AI Act lab

This audit carries forward Case B: AI-assisted CV screening with human review. In the earlier AI Act exercise, the system was classified as high-risk because it ranks applicants and materially influences who progresses to interview, even though a recruiter can confirm or override the shortlist. It is the strongest case for a GDPR audit because it combines profiling, potentially significant employment effects, possible special-category data in free-text CVs, a power imbalance, and a non-EU processor chain. The AI Act and GDPR analyses therefore run in parallel: high-risk product controls do not replace the need for a lawful basis, data minimisation, applicant rights, and valid transfer safeguards.

Fixed fact pattern

TalentBridge GmbH is a fictional Berlin HR-technology company with about 80 employees that provides CV-screening software to medium-sized EU employers. Its employer customers upload approximately 100,000 EU/EEA applications per year, including names, contact details, CVs, education, employment history, skills, languages, cover letters, recruiter notes, and vacancy data. The system extracts job-related information, compares it with pre-set vacancy criteria, assigns a score, and ranks applicants for recruiter review. A recruiter is meant to review the CV, score, and explanation before inviting or rejecting anyone and can override the ranking; the system must not auto-reject. TalentBridge uses an EU cloud host and a US-based model API sub-processor whose EU–US Data Privacy Framework certification has not yet been verified. The intended baseline does not permit applicant data to be used for vendor or product model training, but this must still be confirmed contractually and technically. TalentBridge acts mainly as processor for each employer controller; any independent use for security logs or product improvement requires a separate role and purpose analysis.

CFU checkpoint 1 — Recognize

Personal data present: identity and contact details; education and employment history; skills, languages, location, work eligibility, cover-letter content, recruiter notes; application metadata; AI-generated scores, rankings, explanations, and inferred suitability.

Special-category risk: CVs or attachments may reveal health/disability, trade-union membership, political or religious affiliation, racial or ethnic origin, or other Article 9 information. These data are not necessary for general ranking and must be excluded or segregated.

EU-border crossing: yes. Applicant data may be sent from the EU/EEA processor to a US model API sub-processor.

